In 2025, I offered an amendment that merely would have conditioned a portion of military aid to Israel on their compliance with U.S. law and stated policy goals. That amendment only received 5 votes in support, just 5 Democratic votes. This month, I offered an amendment in committee to prevent the transfer of white phosphorus to Israel, as the IDF has used this dangerous chemical, indiscriminately in densely populated areas in Lebanon. Although it did not pass, 17 Democrats, including the top Democrat on the committee, supported it. My amendment to increase oversight over President Trump's so-called board of peace, saw every single Democrat support it, as well as Republicans, and it actually only failed by one vote. Meanwhile, the block the bombs act in the House of Representatives, which I co-lead with Representative Delia Ramirez, would withhold the transfer of the most egregious weapons to Israel and demand Israel's compliance with U.S. and international law. That bill started with just 21 co-sponsors. But now we are up to 81, and I truly believe we will add many, many more following the Massey amendment. This is a moment of great moral consequence. And one thing we cannot do is forget the faces and the devastation of those being killed in Gaza. We cannot let fade what is actually happening before our very eyes, and with our U.S. taxpayer dollars. We cannot forget the courage of the humanitarian aid workers, the medical professionals, and the courage and the deep resilience and generosity of the people of Gaza, who continue to awe us and inspire us in the face of the enormous brutality they face.
